        // Simpler: we know the reference requires the player to have wagered, so calling play without wager reverts.
        // We'll assert that c.hasPlayerWagered(player) is false after this call (since no wager ever made).
        // On reference: play reverts, so state not changed => hasPlayerWagered(player) is false.
        // On buggy: play modifies state (sets timestamps and wagers to 0) then runs payout/loseWager which sends ether => wagers[player] becomes 0 but the function still runs.
        // Actually after play, wagers[player] is set to 0, so hasPlayerWagered returns false in both cases. Need something else.
        // Let's check address(c).balance: on reference, play reverts so balance unchanged (no ether sent). On buggy, play sends ether to whale or winner, changing balance.
